// Which of the two the camera is pointed at.
//
// **A loaded mesh wins.** A project with real geometry in it is a 3D project,
// whatever else it declares -- the FPS has both a `ui` block and a showroom, and
// what someone opening it wants to see is the showroom. The flat plane is for a
// game that is *only* 2D, which is what a UI and no geometry means.
// Neither branch takes the panel rect any more: the 3D view has d3_viewport and
// the 2D one reads ed_rq(3) through its own fit, so passing it here would be a
// third copy of a number two places already agree on.
ed_content() {
  if (ed_is2d() = 1) {
    ed_plane();
  } else {
    ed_solid();
  }
} return void;

// Asked here and by the navigation, so that the wheel and the middle button move
// whichever view is actually on screen. Two views with one set of bindings is the
// point: a person should not have to know which renderer a project woke up.
ed_is2d() {
} return int bl_ntri() = 0 && (import ed_gu)[0] != 0;
